# Part 3: Model Diagnostics

### Exercise 3.1 {.unnumbered}

Use the `performance` package to check your model. For each diagnostic panel, state what it's testing and whether your model passes or fails.

::: {.callout-tip collapse="true" title="Hint: Which function?"}
`performance::check_model()` produces a panel of diagnostic plots. You can select specific checks with the `check` argument — e.g., `check = c("pp_check", "outliers", "overdispersion")`.
:::


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
performance::check_model(wwwhr_model)
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](Lab_Poisson_Regression_Q_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-13-1.png){width=672}
:::
:::


1.  Posterior predictive check - checks whether the model produces the actual distribution of the observed data. The green dots are widely spread outside of the blue distribution. It fails..
2.  Misspecified dispersion and zero inflation - checks whether the mean is similar enough with the variance. The observed variance is much larger than expected. It fails..
3.  Homogeneity of variance - checks whether residual variance is constant across fitted values. There's a overall upward trend. It fails..
4.  Influential observations - checks if any of the observations in the data was too influential and biased the model. Few dots are outside the lines so Pass?
5.  Collinearity - checks whether the predictors were highly correlated. VIF values are under 2. Pass!
6.  Distribution of quantile residuals - checks whether residuals follow the expected distribution. It deviates a lot from the reference line. It fails..

