### Exercise 3.3 {.unnumbered}

Refit the model without the outliers. Compare the two coefficient tables: which estimates changed substantively, and does the overall story change?

::: {.callout-tip collapse="true" title="Hint: Filtering"}
Store the outlier check as an object, then use it to index your data: `data_clean <- data_pos[!outlier_flag, ]`. Refit the same `glm()` call on the filtered data and compare with `model_parameters()`.
:::


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
outliers_flag <- as.logical(outliers)
data_clean <- data_pos[!outliers_flag, ]
```
:::



::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
wwwhr_clean <- glm(wwwhr ~ wordsum + age + sex + reliten_3f + polviews + wrkhome, 
                   data = data_clean, 
                   family = poisson(link = "log"))

model_parameters(wwwhr_clean) %>% print_html()
```
:::


Polviews coefficient is not significant anymore. Also the effect of sex and wrkhome decreased a lot. Now it seems that several predictors were weaker than expected before, and political views are not related with internet usage.

