# Part 4: Overdispersion & Alternative Models

### Exercise 4.1 {.unnumbered}

Check for overdispersion and report the dispersion ratio. What specific property of the Poisson distribution is violated when the ratio is much greater than 1?

::: {.callout-tip collapse="true" title="Hint: Checking overdispersion"}
`performance::check_overdispersion()` returns a dispersion ratio and a p-value. A ratio near 1 means the Poisson assumption holds.
:::


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
wwwhr_clean <- glm(wwwhr ~ wordsum + age + sex + reliten_3f + polviews + wrkhome, 
                   data = data_clean, 
                   family = poisson(link = "log"))

performance::check_overdispersion(wwwhr_clean)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
# Overdispersion test

       dispersion ratio =   12.119
  Pearson's Chi-Squared = 7174.518
                p-value =  < 0.001
```


:::
:::


Oh no, dispersion ratio is 12.119. It violates the assumption that the variance of the outcome equals the mean.

### Exercise 4.2 {.unnumbered}

Given the overdispersion you found, name a model family that can handle it and explain in 1–2 sentences why it works. Then fit that model to the data and display the results.

::: {.callout-tip collapse="true" title="Hint: An extra parameter"}
The Poisson model has no separate parameter for variance. What if you had a model that added one? Check the `MASS` package.
:::


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
wwwhr_nb <- glm.nb(wwwhr ~ wordsum + age + sex + reliten_3f + polviews + wrkhome, data = data_clean)
model_parameters(wwwhr_nb) %>% print_html()
```

