# Part 8: Report Your Conclusions

### Exercise 8.1 {.unnumbered}

Write a results paragraph following the reporting checklist from the lecture slides. Include a justification for why you used your final model over the Poisson.

::: {.callout-tip collapse="true" title="Hint: Reporting checklist"}
From the lecture: state the hypothesis and model (with link function), report log-count coefficients and IRRs with 95% CIs, include a model fit measure (McFadden R²), mention overdispersion and zero-inflation test results, and note if you switched models and why.
:::

We tested whether cognitive ability, demographics, and religious intensity predict internet use using count regression with a log link. Our initial Poisson model showed substantial overdispersion (dispersion ratio = 12.12, *p* \< .001), so we fitted a negative binomial model. Compared to the Poisson model, model fit strongly favored the negative binomial specification (χ²(1) = 3850.62, *p* \< .001; AIC = 3885.4). In the final model, higher vocabulary scores were associated with greater internet use (beta = 0.10, 95% CI \[0.05, 0.15\], *p* \< .001), while age was negatively associated (beta = −0.01, 95% CI \[-0.02, -0.0007\], *p* \< .001). Religiosity showed a strong positive linear effect (beta = 0.42, 95% CI \[0.25, 0.60\], *p* \< .001). Political views and working from home were not significant.A zero-inflation test showed that the model overpredicted zeros (ratio = 1.61, p \< .001). Overall, the negative binomial model provided the best fit and most reliable inference.
